(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-physiology-master-exam-version-4"]={"title":"Physiology Master Exam — Form D (Final) — Units 6, 7, 12 & 13","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Red blood cells are rich in carbonic anhydrase. Why is this enzyme important for gas transport?","choices":["It keeps hemoglobin's iron in the ferric state so it can bind oxygen","It lets most CO₂ be carried in the blood as bicarbonate","It allows the red cell to generate ATP without mitochondria","It buffers the plasma directly by producing lactic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Carbonic anhydrase catalyzes CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃, which dissociates so most CO₂ travels as bicarbonate (also the body's major extracellular buffer). (A) is wrong — oxygen-binding iron must stay ferrous, and the enzyme doesn't set iron's state. (C) is wrong — RBC ATP comes from glycolytic enzymes. (D) is wrong — the enzyme handles CO₂/bicarbonate, not lactic acid. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 3"},{"q":"Where do granulocytes and monocytes primarily develop, and what happens to most of them before they are needed?","choices":["In lymph nodes; they enter the circulation immediately after forming","In the thymus; they are then held in the spleen until needed","In bone marrow; most are stored there until needed peripherally","In the liver; they finish maturing within the peripheral tissues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Granulocytes and monocytes develop in the bone marrow, where about three times as many are stored as circulate, ready for release. (A), (B), and (D) misplace both the developmental site and the storage pool. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 11"},{"q":"What gives platelets their contractile ability, used later in clot retraction?","choices":["Actin, myosin, and thrombosthenin","Osmotic swelling followed by collapse","Beating of surface cilia","ATP-powered flagellar movement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Platelets contain actin, myosin, and thrombosthenin (actomyosin), giving them true contractile capability. (B), (C), and (D) describe mechanisms platelets do not use — there is no ciliary, flagellar, or purely osmotic contraction. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 32"},{"q":"After entering the tissues, monocytes become macrophages that typically...","choices":["Die within a few hours of arriving","Can survive and function for months or longer","Return to the bloodstream as neutrophils","Convert directly into circulating lymphocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Tissue macrophages are long-lived, surviving months or longer. (A) understates their lifespan, and (C) and (D) describe conversions that do not occur — macrophages do not revert to neutrophils or become lymphocytes. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the chain composition of the predominant adult hemoglobin (hemoglobin A)?","choices":["Four alpha chains","Two alpha and two gamma chains","Two alpha and two beta chains","Two beta and two delta chains"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hemoglobin A, the main adult form, has two alpha and two beta chains. (B) describes fetal-type pairing, and (A) and (D) are combinations not found in normal adult hemoglobin A. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 21"},{"q":"Where do most lymphocytes develop?","choices":["Peripheral lymphoid organs such as the thymus, spleen, and lymph nodes","Exclusively within the red bone marrow of the flat bones and vertebrae","In the liver sinusoids and portal tracts throughout adult life","Within the circulating blood plasma and the interstitial fluid spaces"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lymphocytes develop mostly in peripheral lymphoid organs (thymus, spleen, tonsils, lymph nodes, Peyer's patches). (B) describes granulocyte/monocyte development, and (C) and (D) are not sites of lymphocyte development. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 11"},{"q":"Platelets enter the circulation through which process?","choices":["Budding directly from vascular endothelial cells","Fragmentation of megakaryocytes","Division of circulating monocytes","Final maturation of reticulocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Platelets are cell fragments released by megakaryocytes in the bone marrow. (A), (C), and (D) name unrelated cells — endothelium, monocytes, and reticulocytes (which mature into red cells) do not produce platelets. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which specialized macrophages handle inhaled particles and microorganisms?","choices":["Kupffer cells","Histiocytes","Alveolar macrophages","Splenic macrophages"],"answer":2,"exp":"Alveolar macrophages digest or entrap particles and microbes reaching the lungs. Kupffer cells guard portal blood in the liver, histiocytes are skin/subcutaneous macrophages, and splenic macrophages screen circulating blood. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why is methemoglobin unable to support oxygen transport?","choices":["It binds oxygen so tightly it never releases it","Its iron is in the ferric state and cannot carry oxygen","It has lost the globin chains needed for cooperativity","It precipitates and ruptures the red cell membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Methemoglobin contains ferric (Fe³⁺) iron, which cannot bind and transport oxygen. (A) is wrong — the problem is failure to carry O₂, not to release it. (C) is wrong — globin chains are intact. (D) describes a different process. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 27"},{"q":"The monocyte-macrophage (reticuloendothelial) system mainly functions to...","choices":["Remove dead or abnormal cells, tissues, and foreign substances","Transport oxygen from the lungs out to the peripheral tissues","Produce the antibodies that circulate in the plasma","Generate the clotting factors manufactured within the liver"],"answer":0,"exp":"This distributed system of macrophages clears dead/abnormal cells and foreign material throughout the body. (B) is a red-cell role, (C) is a B-cell/plasma-cell role, and (D) is a hepatocyte role. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why do differences among globin chain types matter functionally?","choices":["They change how many iron atoms each molecule contains","They determine the red cell's lifespan","They set the activity of carbonic anhydrase","They alter the oxygen-binding affinity of hemoglobin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Different globin chains give hemoglobin variants different oxygen-binding affinities. (A) is wrong — each chain still carries one heme/iron. (B) and (C) are unrelated to globin chain identity. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which set of changes characterizes acute inflammation?","choices":["Vasoconstriction, decreased capillary permeability, and leukocyte exclusion","Reduced local blood flow, suppressed immunity, and fewer leukocytes","Vasodilation, increased capillary permeability, and leukocyte accumulation","Increased red-cell production and a fall in the circulating white-cell count"],"answer":2,"exp":"Inflammation features vasodilation with increased blood flow, greater capillary permeability, and accumulation of granulocytes and monocytes. Vasoconstriction with reduced permeability is the opposite, and the reduced-flow/suppression and raised-RBC options misdescribe the response. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 25"},{"q":"In what form is iron transported through the blood plasma?","choices":["Bound to transferrin","Enclosed within ferritin","As free ionic iron","Within hemosiderin granules"],"answer":0,"exp":"Plasma iron is carried loosely bound to transferrin, which can release it to any tissue cell. (B) and (D) are intracellular storage forms, and (C) is wrong — free ionic iron is not the normal transport form. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 25"},{"q":"A distinctive feature of lymphocytes, compared with granulocytes, is that they...","choices":["Live for only a few hours once they reach the circulation","Cannot leave the bloodstream once they have entered it","Are unable to enter the lymph nodes at any stage","Continuously recirculate between blood and lymph and are long-lived"],"answer":3,"exp":"Lymphocytes continuously recirculate blood → lymph → nodes → blood and can live for weeks, months, or longer. (A) describes granulocytes, and (B) and (C) contradict lymphocyte recirculation. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the main intracellular storage form of iron?","choices":["Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin","Ferritin, formed when iron combines with apoferritin","Apotransferrin, the plasma iron carrier"],"answer":2,"exp":"Inside cells, iron combines with apoferritin to form ferritin, the principal storage form. (A) and (D) are transport-related proteins, and (B) is a copper-carrying protein, not an iron store. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 25"},{"q":"What triggers mast cells and basophils to degranulate?","choices":["Direct phagocytosis of a bacterium by the mast cell","Binding of complement C3 to the mast cell surface","Contact between the cell and a rough particle surface","Cross-linking of surface-bound IgE by its specific antigen"],"answer":3,"exp":"When antigen cross-links IgE already bound to their surface receptors, mast cells and basophils degranulate, releasing histamine, heparin, and other mediators. (A), (B), and (C) do not trigger this release. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 34"},{"q":"From the last month of gestation through adulthood, where does blood cell production mainly occur?","choices":["Bone marrow","The yolk sac","The liver and spleen","The lymph nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"By late gestation and throughout adult life, the bone marrow is the primary site of hematopoiesis. The yolk sac (earliest weeks) and liver/spleen (mid-gestation) are earlier sites, and lymph nodes are not the main producers. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 7"},{"q":"Eosinophils are especially important in defense against...","choices":["Intracellular viruses","Encapsulated bacteria","Parasites such as helminths (worms)","Fungal spores circulating in the blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Eosinophils attach to parasites and release toxic substances (including major basic protein) that kill them. They are weak phagocytes, so (A), (B), and (D) are not their specialty. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 33"},{"q":"Where is the majority of erythropoietin produced?","choices":["The bone marrow","The liver","The spleen","The kidney"],"answer":3,"exp":"About 90% of erythropoietin is made in the kidney (roughly 10% in the liver). The bone marrow is EPO's target, not its main source, and the spleen is not a major producer. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 14"},{"q":"According to clonal selection, what happens when an antigen enters the body?","choices":["Every lymphocyte in the body responds to it equally and begins dividing","All circulating lymphocytes rearrange their receptors to match the antigen","It activates the pre-existing clone whose receptors are complementary, which then proliferates","It suppresses further lymphocyte division in order to conserve energy"],"answer":2,"exp":"An antigen selects the pre-existing clone bearing complementary receptors, which then multiplies and secretes matching antibodies. (A) and (B) contradict clonal specificity, and (D) is the opposite of what occurs. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the primary physiological stimulus for erythropoietin release?","choices":["Hypoxia","A rise in blood pH","High iron stores","Increased blood viscosity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Low tissue oxygen (hypoxia) is the key trigger for EPO release. (B), (C), and (D) do not drive EPO; excessive viscosity is a consequence the system tries to avoid, not a stimulus. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which part of an antibody molecule binds to its specific antigen?","choices":["The constant portion of the heavy chains","The disulfide bonds linking the chains","The complement-binding region","The variable portion"],"answer":3,"exp":"The variable portion differs for each antibody and forms the antigen-binding site. The constant portion governs properties like diffusivity and complement binding, disulfide bonds hold the chains together, and the complement region is separate from antigen binding. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which molecule links low oxygen to increased erythropoietin gene transcription?","choices":["Thrombomodulin","Hypoxia-inducible factor-1 (HIF-1)","Carbonic anhydrase in the tubule","Antithrombin III"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hypoxia raises HIF-1, which binds hypoxia-response elements in the EPO promoter to increase EPO transcription. (A) and (D) are coagulation regulators, and (C) is a CO₂-handling enzyme — none control EPO transcription. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which cells provide humoral immunity, and how?","choices":["Cytotoxic T-cells, by lysing infected cells","Macrophages, by phagocytizing antigens","Natural killer cells, by non-specific killing","B-lymphocytes, by forming antibodies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Humoral ('circulating') immunity comes from B-lymphocytes that produce antibodies, while T-cells provide cell-mediated immunity. (A) is cell-mediated, and (B) and (C) are effector mechanisms not classed as humoral immunity. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 40"},{"q":"Why do platelets stick to injured vessel areas but not to normal, intact endothelium?","choices":["Intact endothelium constantly releases thromboxane A2 to attract platelets","Platelets can bind only to nucleated cells","Their surface glycoproteins repel intact endothelium but adhere to exposed collagen","Intact endothelium lacks tissue factor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Platelet surface glycoproteins repel normal intact endothelium yet adhere to injured endothelium and exposed collagen. (A) and (D) misattribute the selectivity, and (B) is false — platelets bind collagen and matrix, not nucleated cells specifically. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 33"},{"q":"Active immunity is produced by...","choices":["Infusing preformed antibodies taken from an already immune donor","Exposure to killed organisms, their products, or attenuated organisms","Blocking activation of the complement cascade","Surgically removing the thymus gland in infancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Active immunity develops when the body is exposed to killed organisms/toxoids or attenuated organisms and mounts its own response. (A) describes passive immunity, and (C) and (D) would impair, not create, immunity. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 63"},{"q":"The relationship between tissue oxygenation, EPO, and red cell mass is an example of what kind of control?","choices":["A positive feedback loop","Feed-forward amplification","An open-loop system","A negative feedback loop"],"answer":3,"exp":"More red cells improve oxygenation, which then reduces the hypoxic drive for EPO — classic negative feedback. (A) would be self-amplifying (the opposite), and (B)/(C) do not describe this self-correcting system. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 13"},{"q":"As a later line of defense, how does the bone marrow respond to a sustained infection?","choices":["It shuts down white-cell production to conserve resources","It increases granulocyte and monocyte production many-fold","It releases mature lymphocytes within minutes","It converts red cells into white cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"The marrow can raise neutrophil and monocyte output 20–50-fold and sustain it for months, with the first new cells released in 3–4 days. (A) is the opposite, and (C) and (D) are physiologically wrong. — Blood and Immunology, Slide 31"},{"q":"How does thrombin act on fibrinogen?","choices":["It cleaves small peptides from fibrinogen, forming fibrin monomers that polymerize","It directly cross-links fibrinogen molecules into a stable insoluble mesh","It converts fibrinogen back into prothrombin","It degrades fibrinogen into soluble fragments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Thrombin removes small peptides from fibrinogen to create fibrin monomers, which polymerize into fibrin fibers. (B) is factor XIII's cross-linking step, (C) reverses the pathway, and (D) describes plasmin's degradative action. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 40"},{"q":"During exercise, more oxygen is released to active muscle largely because...","choices":["Blood flow to muscle stops","Hemoglobin permanently loses its ability to bind oxygen","Tissue PO₂ falls and local acidity/CO₂ promote O₂ unloading","Surfactant increases O₂ release"],"answer":2,"exp":"In exercising muscle, PO₂ drops and the higher CO₂/H⁺ and temperature right-shift the curve (Bohr effect), so hemoglobin releases far more O₂. Hemoglobin doesn't lose function, muscle blood flow rises rather than stops, and surfactant has no role in tissue O₂ release. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is the role of von Willebrand factor in forming the platelet plug?","choices":["It converts circulating prothrombin into thrombin","It helps platelets adhere to exposed collagen","It cross-links fibrin monomers into a mesh","It inhibits platelets to keep the plug small"],"answer":1,"exp":"Von Willebrand factor bridges platelets to collagen, facilitating adhesion. (A) is the job of prothrombin activator, (C) is factor XIII's role, and (D) is the opposite of vWF's pro-adhesive function. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 34"},{"q":"What happens to pleural (intrapleural) pressure during inspiration?","choices":["It becomes more negative (about –5 to –8 cm H₂O)","It stays entirely constant throughout the breath","It becomes positive","It rises toward zero"],"answer":0,"exp":"As the chest expands, intrapleural pressure falls further below atmospheric (roughly –5 to –8 cm H₂O), pulling the lungs open. It does not turn positive or move toward zero during inspiration — those occur in expiration — and it clearly changes across the breath. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 14"},{"q":"What initiates the extrinsic pathway of coagulation?","choices":["Blood contacting collagen or a glass surface","Activation of factor XII","Thrombin cleaving fibrinogen","Release of tissue factor from traumatized tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"The extrinsic pathway begins when injured tissue releases tissue factor (factor III). (A) and (B) start the intrinsic pathway, and (C) is a later, common-pathway event. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 45"},{"q":"Although CO₂ and O₂ have similar molecular weights, CO₂ diffuses much faster mainly because...","choices":["CO₂ is about 20 times more soluble than O₂","CO₂ has a much larger partial pressure gradient","CO₂ is a much smaller molecule","O₂ cannot cross the membrane at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"CO₂'s roughly 20-fold greater solubility lets it diffuse much faster despite a smaller partial pressure gradient. Its gradient is actually smaller, its molecular weight is similar (not smaller), and O₂ obviously does cross the membrane. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 61"},{"q":"In the extrinsic pathway, tissue factor forms a complex that activates which clotting factor?","choices":["Factor XII","Factor VII (converting it to VIIa)","Factor IX","Factor XIII in the plasma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Tissue factor acts as a high-affinity receptor for factor VII, activating it to VIIa; the TF–VIIa complex then activates factor X. (A) and (C) belong to the intrinsic pathway, and (D) is the fibrin-stabilizing factor. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 45"},{"q":"The respiratory system helps regulate blood pH primarily by...","choices":["Adjusting how much CO₂ is exhaled","Producing bicarbonate in the alveoli","Filtering H⁺ out of the blood","Changing hemoglobin concentration"],"answer":0,"exp":"By changing ventilation, the lungs control how much CO₂ is exhaled, shifting the carbonic-acid equilibrium and adjusting pH within minutes. The alveoli don't make bicarbonate or filter H⁺ (a renal function), and hemoglobin level isn't the mechanism. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 91"},{"q":"How does thrombomodulin help limit clotting?","choices":["It directly digests fibrin fibers","It binds thrombin and activates protein C, which inactivates factors Va and VIIIa","It potentiates antithrombin III","It blocks release of tissue factor from the damaged vessel wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thrombomodulin binds thrombin; the complex activates protein C, which inactivates factors Va and VIIIa, slowing coagulation. (A) describes plasmin, (C) describes heparin's mechanism, and (D) is not thrombomodulin's action. — Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation, Slide 48"},{"q":"Lung compliance is best defined as...","choices":["The pressure needed to overcome resistance in the conducting airways","The tendency of the lung to recoil after stretch","The rate of airflow through the bronchioles","The change in lung volume per unit change in pressure (ΔV/ΔP)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Compliance measures how much lung volume changes for a given change in pressure (ΔV/ΔP) — its distensibility. Recoil tendency is elasticity (the opposite idea), while airway resistance and airflow rate describe how easily air moves, not how easily the lung stretches. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 14"},{"q":"Enterokinase (enteropeptidase) on the intestinal brush border is essential because it...","choices":["Activates trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates the other pancreatic zymogens","Directly digests the dietary triglycerides present in the duodenal lumen","Neutralizes gastric acid arriving from the stomach","Absorbs free amino acids across the brush border"],"answer":0,"exp":"Enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, and more trypsinogen. It does not digest fat, neutralize acid, or absorb amino acids. — GI Physiology, Slide 104"},{"q":"What is the difference between a lung “volume” and a lung “capacity”?","choices":["A capacity is always smaller than a single volume","A capacity is the sum of two or more lung volumes","They are two words for the same thing","A volume can only be measured during exercise"],"answer":1,"exp":"A lung capacity is defined as the sum of two or more lung volumes (e.g., vital capacity = IRV + TV + ERV). Capacities are therefore larger than individual volumes, are not synonymous with them, and are measured at rest. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 27"},{"q":"After fats are reassembled into triglycerides and packaged into chylomicrons, they enter the...","choices":["Portal blood directly, and travel straight onward to the liver for processing","Bile ducts, and return to the gallbladder","Lymphatic system (lacteals), because chylomicrons are too large for blood capillaries","Gastric veins, draining back toward the stomach"],"answer":2,"exp":"Chylomicrons are too large to enter blood capillaries, so they pass into the lacteals and travel via lymph to the thoracic duct before reaching the blood. They do not enter portal blood, bile ducts, or gastric veins directly. — GI Physiology, Slide 135"},{"q":"How does a decrease in lung volume affect airway resistance?","choices":["It increases resistance","It decreases resistance","It eliminates resistance","It has no effect on resistance"],"answer":0,"exp":"As lung volume falls, the airways are less stretched open and narrow, so airway resistance rises. Resistance is never eliminated, and it clearly depends on lung volume, so “no effect” is incorrect. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 16"},{"q":"Bile, which is essential for fat emulsification, is produced by which organ?","choices":["The gallbladder","The liver","The pancreas","The duodenal mucosa"],"answer":1,"exp":"The liver produces bile continuously; the gallbladder only stores and concentrates it between meals. The pancreas and duodenal mucosa secrete other digestive fluids, not bile. — GI Physiology, Slide 113"},{"q":"Inspiratory capacity is the maximum air that can be inspired following...","choices":["A maximal inspiration","A maximal expiration","A normal (resting) expiration","A forced exhalation to residual volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"Inspiratory capacity (= TV + IRV) is the most air inhaled starting from the end of a normal, resting expiration. Starting from a maximal expiration or residual volume would approach total lung capacity, and you cannot inhale further after a maximal inspiration. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 28"},{"q":"How does the spinal-cord (parasympathetic) defecation reflex relate to the intrinsic reflex?","choices":["It replaces the intrinsic myenteric reflex entirely once it fires","It is qualitatively different from the intrinsic reflex and unrelated to it","It inhibits defecation by relaxing the rectal wall","It greatly intensifies the intrinsic reflex via pelvic-nerve parasympathetic fibers"],"answer":3,"exp":"The spinal (parasympathetic, S2–S4) reflex reinforces — not replaces — the intrinsic reflex, greatly strengthening colonic and rectal contractions through pelvic-nerve fibers. It is not qualitatively different, unrelated, or inhibitory. — GI Physiology, Slide 67"},{"q":"When arterial PO₂ falls substantially (PCO₂ held constant), ventilation...","choices":["Increases, driven by the peripheral chemoreceptors","Stays at exactly the same rate as it was before","Decreases","Stops entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"A substantial fall in PO₂ stimulates the carotid and aortic bodies, increasing ventilation (the hypoxic drive). Ventilation does not fall, stay flat, or stop when oxygen drops. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 99"},{"q":"The migrating motor complex (MMC) functions to...","choices":["Mix chyme thoroughly with the digestive enzymes during the hour right after a meal is eaten","Sweep undigested residue toward the colon between meals, keeping upper-gut bacterial counts low","Trigger the mass movements that sweep material through the colon","Neutralize the gastric acid arriving in the duodenum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The MMC is the interdigestive “housekeeping” pattern that clears residue and bacteria from the upper gut between meals, mediated by motilin. It is not a postprandial mixing pattern, does not drive colonic mass movements, and has no role in neutralizing acid. — GI Physiology, Slide 55"},{"q":"Respiratory regulation is organized as which three-part system?","choices":["Surfactant, mucus, and cilia","Arteries, veins, capillaries, and lymphatics","Alveoli, bronchioles, and pleura","Sensors, a central controller, and effector muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Respiratory control is a feedback loop: sensors (chemoreceptors) gather data, the central controller (brainstem) integrates it, and effectors (respiratory muscles) act. The other trios list vascular, structural, or airway-lining parts, not the control loop. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 88"},{"q":"Peristalsis (the propulsive movement of the gut) fundamentally requires...","choices":["The myenteric (Auerbach's) plexus, and can occur without the autonomic nervous system","The sphincter of Oddi to open first, before any contraction wave can begin","The sympathetic nervous system to initiate and sustain each contraction wave","Complete removal of the enteric nervous system before propulsion can occur"],"answer":0,"exp":"Peristalsis is coordinated by the enteric (myenteric) plexus and proceeds even without autonomic input, though the parasympathetics modulate it. It does not require the sympathetic system to start, the sphincter of Oddi, or removal of the ENS — destroying the ENS actually abolishes it. — GI Physiology, Slide 39"},{"q":"About how much oxygen can each gram of hemoglobin bind?","choices":["0.3 ml O₂","1.34 ml O₂","100 ml O₂","20 ml O₂"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each gram of hemoglobin binds about 1.34 ml of O₂. The 0.3 ml figure is dissolved O₂ per 100 ml of blood, and ~20 ml is the total O₂-carrying capacity per 100 ml — not the per-gram value. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which gastric cell secretes both hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor secreted by the gastric mucosa?","choices":["Chief (peptic) cells","G-cells","Parietal (oxyntic) cells","Mucous neck cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete both HCl and intrinsic factor secreted by the gastric mucosa. Chief cells make pepsinogen, G-cells make gastrin, and mucous neck cells make mucus. — GI Physiology, Slide 79"},{"q":"As bicarbonate leaves the red cell in exchange for chloride entering, this exchange is called the...","choices":["Bohr shift","The Haldane shift effect","Sodium shift","Chloride (Hamburger) shift"],"answer":3,"exp":"As bicarbonate leaves the red cell, chloride moves in to preserve electrical neutrality — the chloride (Hamburger) shift. The Bohr and Haldane effects describe how pH/O₂ change gas affinities, and there is no “sodium shift” here. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 86"},{"q":"Besides stimulating gastric acid secretion, gastrin has what other notable action?","choices":["A trophic effect promoting growth of the oxyntic (gastric) mucosa","Emptying the gallbladder of stored bile","Stimulating secretion of enzymes from the pancreatic acinar cells","Inhibiting every form of gastric secretion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Beyond stimulating acid, gastrin is trophic to the oxyntic mucosa, promoting its growth. Gallbladder emptying and pancreatic enzyme secretion are CCK's actions, and gastrin stimulates rather than inhibits gastric secretion. — GI Physiology, Slide 12"},{"q":"Carbon monoxide is dangerous to oxygen transport because it...","choices":["Increases the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood markedly","Cannot bind hemoglobin at all","Binds hemoglobin far more tightly than O₂, blocking O₂ binding","Only affects dissolved oxygen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin with far greater affinity than oxygen, occupying binding sites and preventing O₂ transport. It reduces (not increases) usable capacity, definitely binds hemoglobin, and acts on hemoglobin rather than dissolved O₂. — Pulmonary Physiology I & II, Slide 82"},{"q":"When the antral pH becomes very low, gastrin release is inhibited largely because acid...","choices":["Directly destroys the antral G-cells that make gastrin","Increases histamine release from the enterochromaffin-like cells nearby","Converts circulating gastrin into secretin in the duodenum","Stimulates somatostatin release, which suppresses gastrin (negative feedback)"],"answer":3,"exp":"A low antral pH stimulates somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin release — a negative-feedback brake on acid secretion. Acid does not destroy G-cells, convert gastrin to secretin, or raise histamine. — GI Physiology, Slide 94"},{"q":"Glucose enters most cells of the body by...","choices":["Primary active transport that directly uses ATP","Facilitated diffusion — a carrier is needed but energy is not","Simple diffusion straight through the lipid bilayer, unaided","Receptor-mediated endocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Glucose crosses most cell membranes by facilitated diffusion: a carrier protein (GLUT) moves it down its concentration gradient without using ATP. It is not pumped by primary active transport, does not dissolve through the bilayer, and is not taken up by endocytosis. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 68, Slide 4"},{"q":"Once formed, trypsin plays a central role in the intestine by...","choices":["Emulsifying dietary fats within the small intestine","Neutralizing the acidic chyme entering the duodenum","Activating chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, and more trypsinogen","Absorbing the di- and tripeptides across the intestinal brush border"],"answer":2,"exp":"Trypsin is the master activator: it converts chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, and additional trypsinogen to their active forms. Neutralizing acid is bicarbonate's role, emulsifying fat is bile's, and absorption is the mucosa's. — GI Physiology, Slide 104"},{"q":"Chylomicrons and VLDL differ in that...","choices":["both are made only in the liver","neither particle carries triglyceride","chylomicrons are assembled in the liver, while VLDL is made in the gut wall","chylomicrons carry dietary fat from the intestine, while VLDL is made in the liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Chylomicrons are assembled in intestinal mucosa to carry absorbed dietary fat, while VLDL is synthesized in the liver (its triglyceride made largely from carbohydrate). The other options misplace their origins or deny that they carry triglyceride. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 69, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which hormone is released cyclically during fasting to drive the migrating motor complexes?","choices":["Cholecystokinin","Motilin","Secretin","Gastrin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Motilin is released cyclically during fasting and drives the migrating motor complexes. CCK, secretin, and gastrin are released in response to a meal and serve digestive rather than interdigestive roles. — GI Physiology, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which condition favors gluconeogenesis?","choices":["High blood glucose right after a large meal","A high insulin-to-glucagon ratio","A high glucagon-to-insulin ratio (as in fasting)","Low cortisol levels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gluconeogenesis (mainly hepatic) is favored by a high glucagon:insulin ratio, high mitochondrial NADH and acetyl-CoA, and high cortisol — the fasting state. A high insulin ratio, post-meal hyperglycemia, and low cortisol all suppress it. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 68, Slide 30"},{"q":"The villi and microvilli of the small intestine are important because they...","choices":["Secrete the digestive enzymes made by the pancreas","Store bile salts until the next meal arrives","Neutralize the gastric acid arriving in the duodenum","Vastly increase the surface area available for absorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Villi (~10-fold) and microvilli/brush border (~20-fold) hugely amplify the absorptive surface area of the small intestine. They do not secrete pancreatic enzymes, neutralize acid, or store bile salts. — GI Physiology, Slide 118"},{"q":"Insulin promotes fat storage by...","choices":["stimulating lipoprotein lipase and inhibiting hormone-sensitive lipase","blocking glucose uptake into cells","stimulating hormone-sensitive lipase so that stored fat is released","promoting beta-oxidation of fat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Insulin stores fat by activating lipoprotein lipase (loading fatty acids into adipocytes) and inhibiting hormone-sensitive lipase (blocking breakdown of stored triglyceride). It promotes glucose uptake and opposes fat mobilization and beta-oxidation. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 69, Slide 22"},{"q":"Vitamin B12 (bound to intrinsic factor secreted by the gastric mucosa) is absorbed at which site?","choices":["The ileum","The duodenum","The colon","The stomach"],"answer":0,"exp":"The intrinsic-factor–B12 complex is absorbed in the ileum (as are bile salts). The stomach and colon absorb little B12, and the duodenum mainly absorbs other nutrients, iron, and calcium. — GI Physiology, Slide 121"},{"q":"Oxidative deamination of an amino acid removes the amino group, generating...","choices":["glucose","ammonia, which must be detoxified","a ketone body for use as fuel","a fatty acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Deamination strips the amino group off an amino acid, releasing ammonia (which the liver converts to urea). It does not directly yield glucose, a fatty acid, or a ketone body — those may come from the remaining carbon skeleton. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 70, Slide 16"},{"q":"Regarding the liver's blood supply...","choices":["It is supplied almost entirely by the hepatic artery, not the portal vein","It receives less than 5% of cardiac output","It receives about 30% of cardiac output, most of it from the portal vein","It has no venous inflow"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 30% of cardiac output flows through the liver, roughly 75% via the portal vein and 25% via the hepatic artery, at low pressure and resistance. It is not chiefly arterial, is far more than 5% of output, and has major portal venous inflow. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 71, Slide 3"},{"q":"In the enterohepatic circulation, about 95% of secreted bile salts are...","choices":["excreted in the feces","destroyed by pancreatic enzymes","converted back into free cholesterol within the intestinal lumen","reabsorbed (mostly in the ileum) and returned to the liver for reuse"],"answer":3,"exp":"About 95% of secreted bile salts are reabsorbed, chiefly in the ileum, and returned via portal blood to the liver; only the small fraction lost in feces is replaced by new synthesis. They are not mostly excreted, digested, or turned back into cholesterol in the gut. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 71, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which macronutrient provides the most energy per gram?","choices":["Fat (~9 kcal/g)","Carbohydrate (~4 kcal/g)","Protein (~4 kcal/g)","All provide equal energy per gram"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fat yields about 9 kcal/g, more than double carbohydrate or protein (~4 kcal/g each). They are not equal per gram. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 72, Slide 4"},{"q":"Vitamin D is essential for...","choices":["collagen synthesis","calcium and phosphate balance","amino acid transamination","oxygen transport"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vitamin D (activated via skin UV, then the liver and kidney) is essential for calcium and phosphate balance; deficiency causes rickets. Collagen needs vitamin C, oxygen transport needs hemoglobin, and transamination needs vitamin B6. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 72, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which nutrient produces the greatest thermic effect (rise in metabolic rate during digestion)?","choices":["Fat (~4%)","Carbohydrate (~4%)","Protein (up to ~30%)","All are equal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Protein has by far the largest thermic effect of food, raising metabolic rate up to ~30%, versus about 4% for carbohydrate and fat. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 73, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which is a mechanism of heat LOSS from the body?","choices":["Piloerection","Shivering","Vasoconstriction of skin vessels","Evaporation of sweat"],"answer":3,"exp":"Evaporation (of sweat and insensible water) loses heat, along with radiation and conduction/convection. Shivering and piloerection generate or conserve heat, and skin vasoconstriction reduces heat loss. — Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Ch. 74, Slide 6"}]};
